        <li><strong>Single Applicant Baseline</strong>: Proof of at least <strong>€3,500 net per month</strong> (after foreign taxes and statutory deductions).</li>
        <li><strong>Spouse / Registered Civil Partner Surcharge</strong>: The required monthly threshold increases by <strong>20% (+€700)</strong>, bringing the family threshold to <strong>€4,200 net/month</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Dependent Children Surcharge</strong>: Each minor dependent child adds <strong>15% (+€525)</strong> to the baseline monthly threshold. For example, a family of four (applicant, spouse, two children) requires <code>€3,500 + €700 + €525 + €525 = €5,250 net/month</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Consulates require evidentiary documentation certified with the <strong>Hague Apostille</strong> (or consular legalization for non-Apostille states) accompanied by certified official translations into modern Greek:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Remote Employment Contract</strong>: For employees, an open-ended employment contract (or fixed-term contract with at least 12 months remaining) specifying full remote duties performed via telecommunications tools.</li>
        <li><strong>Freelance Service Master Agreements</strong>: For contractors, written client agreements detailing consulting rates, scope of deliverables, and proof that no client is a Greek tax resident.</li>
        <li><strong>6-Month International Bank Statements</strong>: Official bank transcripts showing consistent incoming wire transfers matching or exceeding the required net amount.</li>
        <li><strong>Company Chamber of Commerce Certificate</strong>: For business owners operating through their own foreign entity (e.g. US LLC, UK Ltd, Delaware C-Corp), official corporate registry extracts confirming beneficial ownership of 100% of share capital.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Social Security &amp; Pension Liabilities: Defeating the EFKA Contribution Trap
      </h2>
      <p>
        The single most dangerous financial hazard facing international nomads moving to Greece is the national social insurance organization, <strong>EFKA (Unified Social Security Entity)</strong>. Standard Greek freelancers pay mandatory monthly contributions ranging from €240 (Class 1) to €630 (Class 6), plus mandatory health and auxiliary pension insurance.
      </p>
      <p>
        To legally bypass EFKA liability, remote workers must deploy bilateral treaty protections:
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-4 my-4">
        <div class="bg-slate-900/80 p-5 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-amber-400 mb-2">1. EU/EEA Citizens &amp; Posted Workers: The A1 Certificate</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Under EU Regulation 883/2004 (Article 12), employees of EU companies working remotely from another member state can remain insured under their home country's social security scheme. You must obtain an <strong>A1 Portable Document</strong> from your home social security administration prior to relocation. With an active A1, Greek authorities have zero legal jurisdiction to assess EFKA contributions.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900/80 p-5 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-2">2. United States Citizens: US-Greece Bilateral Totalization Agreement</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The United States and Greece maintain a bilateral Social Security Totalization Agreement (in force since 1994). US remote employees and sole proprietors who continue paying US Self-Employment Tax (SECA) or FICA payroll deductions can request an official <strong>Certificate of Coverage</strong> from the US Social Security Administration (SSA Form SSA-2975). Filing this certificate with the Greek Ministry of Labor grants a 100% legal exemption from EFKA payroll taxes.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900/80 p-5 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
