        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Digital Nomad Residence Card Receipt</strong>:
          Within 60 to 90 days, collect your 2-year biometric residence permit card. This permit is renewable for consecutive 2-year increments as long as you maintain the €3,500/month foreign income criteria.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Filing Form D211 for Article 5C: The Critical March 31 Deadline &amp; Pitfalls
      </h2>
      <p>
        Holding a Greek Digital Nomad Visa does <strong>not</strong> automatically activate the 50% tax exemption. Article 5C is an independent tax incentive procedure administered strictly by the Independent Authority for Public Revenue (<strong>AADE</strong>).
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-900/60 p-6 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-rose-500 mb-6 text-slate-200">
        <p class="font-bold text-rose-400 mb-1">Fatal Procedural Pitfall: The March 31 Hard Deadline</p>
        <p class="text-sm">
          To qualify for the 50% exemption, you must submit <strong>Form D211 (Application for Transfer of Tax Residency under Article 5C)</strong> to the Tax Office for Foreign Residents (DOY Katoikon Exoterikou) no later than <strong>March 31st</strong> of the calendar year immediately following the year in which you established tax residency in Greece. If you relocate in 2026, your deadline is March 31, 2027. Failure to meet this filing deadline permanently disqualifies you for that tax year.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        To secure approval under Article 5C, you must substantiate three statutory prerequisites:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Prior 5-Year Non-Residency Proof</strong>: You must establish that you were not a tax resident of Greece for <strong>5 out of the 6 tax years</strong> preceding the year of your relocation. This is verified via official tax certificates from your former home country.</li>
        <li><strong>Relocation from Eligible State</strong>: You must relocate from an EU/EEA member state or a nation with which Greece has an active administrative assistance agreement for tax matters.</li>
        <li><strong>Mandatory 2-Year Physical Residence Commitment</strong>: You must formally commit to maintaining your primary tax residence in Greece for at least <strong>two consecutive tax years</strong>. Leaving before 24 months revokes your exemption retroactively, triggering clawbacks of standard progressive tax rates plus statutory interest.</li>
      </ul>

