      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Interactive Greece Digital Nomad Tax Savings Calculator
      </h2>
      <p>
        Use the client-side calculator below to model your personalized Greek tax liability and compare your savings under Law 4758 against standard resident taxation:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-slate-900/80 p-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-700 my-6">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
          <div>
            <label class="block text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 mb-1">Gross Annual Income (€ EUR)</label>
            <input 
              type="number" 
              id="calc-salary" 
              value="85000" 
              step="5000" 
              min="42000" 
              class="w-full bg-slate-950 border border-slate-700 rounded p-2 text-sm text-white font-mono focus:border-amber-500 focus:outline-none"
            />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 mb-1">Family Status</label>
            <select id="calc-family" class="w-full bg-slate-950 border border-slate-700 rounded p-2 text-sm text-white font-mono focus:border-amber-500 focus:outline-none">
              <option value="single" selected>Single Remote Worker</option>
              <option value="couple">Married (Spouse)</option>
              <option value="family1">Married + 1 Child</option>
              <option value="family2">Married + 2 Children</option>
            </select>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 p-4 bg-slate-950/80 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 text-center">
          <div>
            <span class="block text-xs text-slate-400 font-mono">Standard Greek Tax</span>
            <span id="res-std-tax" class="text-xl font-bold text-rose-400">€29,700</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block text-xs text-slate-400 font-mono">Law 4758 Tax Liability</span>
            <span id="res-law-tax" class="text-xl font-bold text-emerald-400">€11,700</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block text-xs text-slate-400 font-mono">Effective Tax Rate</span>
            <span id="res-eff-rate" class="text-xl font-bold text-amber-400">13.8%</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block text-xs text-slate-400 font-mono">Annual Net Savings</span>
            <span id="res-savings" class="text-xl font-bold text-emerald-300">€18,000 / yr</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mt-3 font-mono">
          *Calculations reflect statutory Greek progressive brackets applied against 50% taxable base under Law 4758/2020 Article 5C.
        </p>
      </div>

      <script is:inline>
        function calculateGreekTax(taxableIncome) {
          let tax = 0;
          if (taxableIncome <= 10000) {
            tax = taxableIncome * 0.09;
          } else if (taxableIncome <= 20000) {
            tax = (10000 * 0.09) + ((taxableIncome - 10000) * 0.22);
          } else if (taxableIncome <= 30000) {
            tax = (10000 * 0.09) + (10000 * 0.22) + ((taxableIncome - 20000) * 0.28);
          } else if (taxableIncome <= 40000) {
            tax = (10000 * 0.09) + (10000 * 0.22) + (10000 * 0.28) + ((taxableIncome - 30000) * 0.36);
          } else {
            tax = (10000 * 0.09) + (10000 * 0.22) + (10000 * 0.28) + (10000 * 0.36) + ((taxableIncome - 40000) * 0.44);
          }
          return tax;
        }

        function recalculateNomadTax() {
          const gross = parseFloat(document.getElementById('calc-salary').value) || 0;
          if (gross <= 0) return;

          const standardTax = calculateGreekTax(gross);
          const lawTaxable = gross * 0.50; // 50% statutory reduction
          const lawTax = calculateGreekTax(lawTaxable);
          const annualSavings = standardTax - lawTax;
          const effectiveRate = (lawTax / gross) * 100;

          document.getElementById('res-std-tax').textContent = "€" + Math.round(standardTax).toLocaleString();
          document.getElementById('res-law-tax').textContent = "€" + Math.round(lawTax).toLocaleString();
          document.getElementById('res-eff-rate').textContent = effectiveRate.toFixed(1) + "%";
          document.getElementById('res-savings').textContent = "€" + Math.round(annualSavings).toLocaleString() + " / yr";
        }

        document.getElementById('calc-salary').addEventListener('input', recalculateNomadTax);
        document.getElementById('calc-family').addEventListener('change', recalculateNomadTax);
      </script>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Double Taxation Treaties (DTT) &amp; 183-Day Permanent Establishment Safeguards
      </h2>
      <p>
        Relocating tax residency to Greece triggers international tax implications under bilateral Double Taxation Treaties (DTTs) modeled on the OECD Convention. To safeguard both your personal position and your foreign employer:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-3 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>OECD Article 4 Tie-Breaker Rules</strong>: If your home country asserts continuing tax residency (e.g. the UK statutory residence test or California resident ties), treaty tie-breaker rules prioritize the location of your <em>permanent home</em> and the <em>center of your vital economic and social interests</em>. Registering an apartment lease in Greece, moving your family, and acquiring local private health insurance establishes vital interests firmly in Greece.</li>
        <li><strong>Preventing Corporate Permanent Establishment (PE)</strong>: Foreign employers often worry that an employee working remotely in Greece creates a taxable corporate presence (PE) under OECD Article 5. To eliminate PE risk, remote employment agreements must explicitly state that the employee does <strong>not</strong> hold commercial negotiation authority, cannot bind the company to sales contracts, and performs purely preparatory or auxiliary technical tasks.</li>
        <li><strong>US Citizens &amp; The Foreign Earned Income Exclusion (FEIE)</strong>: American digital nomads can combine Greece's 50% tax break with the US FEIE (Form 2555), excluding up to $126,500 of foreign earned income from US federal taxes. Alternatively, use the Foreign Tax Credit (Form 1116) to credit Greek taxes against any remaining US liabilities.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        In conclusion: Greece's Law 4758/2020 delivers one of the most reliable, legally secure, and financially rewarding frameworks for international remote workers in 2026. By navigating EFKA exemptions via bilateral treaties, adhering to the €3,500/month solvency rules, and filing Form D211 prior to March 31st, tech nomads can legally halve their income tax liability for seven full years while enjoying premier Mediterranean lifestyle quality.
      </p>
